From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sr-0w <Sr-0w@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 02:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: camss: report VFE interrupt status during bring-up

Capture on this port returns correctly sized buffers containing only zeroes,
while every stage checkable from userspace is configured correctly: the sensor
streams at the right mode, the CSIPHY runs the right generation's sequence with
the right settle count, the CSID decodes RAW10 payload-only from the right PHY,
and the SMMU does not fault.

The remaining question is what the VFE interrupt actually reports. A steady
30 fps looked like start-of-frame arriving from the CSID, but a second write
master fed from the same CSID blocks entirely, which makes that reading unsafe.

Print the interrupt and bus status for the first few interrupts of a session so
the two can be told apart. Temporary, to be dropped once frames arrive.

Signed-off-by: Sr-0w <Sr-0w@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe-17x.c | 32 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe-17x.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe-17x.c
@@ -260,6 +260,22 @@
 		&line->video_out.active_fmt.fmt.pix_mp;
 	u32 stride = pix->plane_fmt[0].bytesperline;
 
+	/*
+	 * Bring-up instrumentation. The write master runs while its image
+	 * address register reads back zero, and the address written here is
+	 * the only thing that sets it, so report what is actually programmed.
+	 */
+	{
+		static int reported;
+
+		if (reported < 6) {
+			reported++;
+			dev_info(vfe->camss->dev,
+				 "vfe%d wm%u: addr=%08x stride=%u height=%u\n",
+				 vfe->id, wm, addr, stride, pix->height);
+		}
+	}
+
 	writel_relaxed(addr, vfe->base + VFE_BUS_WM_IMAGE_ADDR(wm));
 	writel_relaxed(stride * pix->height, vfe->base + VFE_BUS_WM_FRAME_INC(wm));
 }
@@ -348,6 +364,27 @@
 	writel_relaxed(CMD_GLOBAL_CLEAR, vfe->base + VFE_IRQ_CMD);
 	writel_relaxed(1, vfe->base + VFE_BUS_IRQ_CLEAR_GLOBAL);
 
+	/*
+	 * Bring-up instrumentation. Capture returns correctly sized buffers
+	 * full of zeroes while every stage checkable from userspace is
+	 * configured correctly, so the question is what this interrupt really
+	 * reports. Reset acknowledgements dominate and say nothing, so only
+	 * interrupts carrying something else are printed, and only a few.
+	 */
+	if ((status0 & ~STATUS_0_RESET_ACK) || status1 ||
+	    vfe_bus_status[VFE_LINE_RDI0] || vfe_bus_status[VFE_LINE_RDI1]) {
+		static int reported;
+
+		if (reported < 24) {
+			reported++;
+			dev_info(vfe->camss->dev,
+				 "vfe%d irq: status0=%08x status1=%08x bus_rdi0=%08x bus_rdi1=%08x\n",
+				 vfe->id, status0, status1,
+				 vfe_bus_status[VFE_LINE_RDI0],
+				 vfe_bus_status[VFE_LINE_RDI1]);
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (status0 & STATUS_0_RESET_ACK)
 		vfe->isr_ops.reset_ack(vfe);
 
--
2.50.1
